+/// Per-session native tool denylist applied to every session this bridge
+/// serves (`session/create` / `session/resume` `toolDenylist`).
+///
+/// Root cause this closes (ZCode 0.16.5 App bundle, verified read-only):
+/// the native `PermissionService` `checkBuildMode` auto-allows any tool call
+/// whose capability resolves `readOnly && !destructive && !needsApproval`,
+/// and the native safe-command table classifies e.g. `["ls", {allowAnyArgs:
+/// true}]` as read-only — so a Worker's `ls tests/fixtures` executed with no
+/// `interaction/requestPermission`, bypassing the ACP host's permission
+/// packet (observed as D1Q-02 attempt d1q02-r1 events seq 8/9).
+///
+/// The native protocol cannot express "ask for every Bash call" per session:
+/// the `session/create` mode enum (plan/build/edit/yolo/auto) has no
+/// ask-enforcing mode (plan and build both auto-allow read-only tools),
+/// runtime preferences carry no permission fields, and project "ask" rules
+/// (`rule.project.ask`) live in the persisted session database, reachable
+/// only via permission-response updates that cannot cover a session's first
+/// Bash call. The documented per-session policy that deterministically
+/// removes native shell execution — including subagent children, which
+/// inherit `toolDisallowlist` — is the create/resume denylist, so Bash is
+/// unregistered for the model entirely. Honest limitation: an ACP host can
+/// no longer grant shell through this bridge; every other tool keeps its
+/// native ask semantics and stays host-mediated.
+const SHELL_TOOL_DENYLIST: &[&str] = &["Bash"];
